\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Three inequivalent selectors and their observables}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{On the unit four-cycle with #{m=1}, the
[opposite, adjacent and positive half-turn selectors](fdip-005F)
are pairwise inequivalent under the orthogonal comparison.
Consequently no directed or signed graph arrow identifies any pair.
Their retained precisions have the following invariants:}
##{\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
\text{selector}&\operatorname{spec}S_P&\det S_P&\trace S_P\\\hline
\text{opposite}&\{5/3,3\}&5&14/3\\
\text{adjacent}&\{3/2,15/4\}&45/8&21/4\\
\text{half-turn}&\{1,5\}&5&6.
\end{array}}

\proof{
\p{The three two-by-two precisions and their eigenvectors are
computed in \ref{fdip-005F}. Their distinct spectra separate them
by the [invariance proposition](fdip-005J). Products and sums of
the eigenvalues give the displayed determinants and traces.}

\p{Let #{i_X:X\to H} be the inclusion of the retained space. In an
orthonormal basis of #{X}, the squared norm is the sum of squares
of coordinates, and each coordinate has variance equal to the
corresponding diagonal entry of #{C_P}. Since #{C_P=i_X^*\,G\,i_X}
and #{P^2=P}, cyclicity of the finite-dimensional trace gives
#{\trace_X C_P=\trace_H(PG)}.}
}

\p{The intrinsic squared-norm observable has expectation
##{\mathbb E_{\mu_Q}\|P\phi\|^2
 =\mathbb E_{\nu_b}\|x\|^2
 =\trace_X C_P
 =\trace_H(PG).}
In the same order as the table,
##{\mathbb E\|P\phi\|^2=\frac{14}{15},\quad
\frac{14}{15},\quad\frac65.}
The reciprocal eigenvalues give these values. Thus neither
#{\det S_P} nor this covariance trace alone separates all three
selectors. In particular the equal covariance traces of the first
two do not mean equal precision traces.}

\p{For the two coordinate selectors, named coordinate observations
also distinguish the laws. The full covariance in \ref{fdip-005F}
gives
##{\begin{aligned}
\frac{\langle\phi_0\phi_2\rangle}{\langle\phi_0^2\rangle}
 &=\frac27&&\text{(opposite)},\\
\frac{\langle\phi_0\phi_1\rangle}{\langle\phi_0^2\rangle}
 &=\frac37&&\text{(adjacent)}.
\end{aligned}}
The marginal expectation identity makes these the corresponding
retained expectations. They refer to the stated vertex coordinates;
unlike the spectrum and squared norm, such coordinate ratios are
not invariants under arbitrary orthogonal changes of retained basis.}
